# Keep the e2e build context to the source the image actually builds. Two
# reasons beyond speed: a stale chain.db or peers.json copied into the image
# would make a "fresh" node in the container start with someone else's state,
# and wallet.json is a private key that has no business inside an image. What
# reaches the build is what the image is a function of, so anything the build
# does not compile is excluded — the fewer inputs, the more a rebuild can be
# trusted to reproduce.

.git
.github

# Build outputs
bin/
dist/
/dnas
/dnas-tui
/dnas-race

# Node/wallet state from local runs
*.db
*.db.tmp
wallet.json
wallet*.json
w*.json
alice.json
bob.json
peers.json
bans.json
mempool.json
spvwallet.json
node.json

# A node's IDENTITY key is a private key too — the same argument as wallet.json
# above — and so is a backup bundle, encrypted or not.
nodekey*.json
dnas-backup*.json

# Light-client caches, the files the multi-party signing flows pass between
# signers, and anchor receipts. None of them is compiled, and a half-signed
# spend copied into an image is somebody's pending payment.
spvheaders*.json
*.headers
multisig-spend*.json
escrow*.json
sponsored-tx*.json
invoice*.json
*.dnasanchor

# Everything else the docs have a reader create at the root by name (m1.json,
# cold.json, spend.json, sig.json, …). The build compiles Go; it needs no JSON
# from the root at all, so excluding the lot keeps the context a function of the
# source rather than of whatever the last local run left behind.
/*.json

# Not part of the e2e image: tui/ is its own module with external dependencies
# and is absent from go.work, gui/ is Python. Neither is compiled here.
gui/
tui/

# Editor / tooling noise
.claude
__pycache__/
*.pyc
